11
00:00:28,662 --> 00:00:34,337
Preeclampsia remains one of the leading causes of death and severe maternal morbidity.

12
00:00:34,337 --> 00:00:41,487
This chapter discusses many clinical aspects of the preeclampsia syndrome and its management after recognition.

13
00:00:41,487 --> 00:00:46,650
Also presented are the longterm consequences that may accrue in affected women.

14
00:00:46,650 --> 00:00:51,537
The pathophysiology of preeclampsia was detailed in Chapter 40.

15
00:00:51,537 --> 00:00:54,325
Diagnosis And Evaluation.

16
00:00:54,325 --> 00:00:56,725
# Diagnosis.

17
00:00:56,725 --> 00:01:05,300
In routine prenatal care, gravidas are seen more often during the third trimester, and this aids early detection of preeclampsia.

18
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:09,312
However, it cannot always be diagnosed definitively.

19
00:01:09,312 --> 00:01:17,662
Increases in systolic and diastolic blood pressure can be either normal physiological changes or signs of developing pathology.

20
00:01:17,662 --> 00:01:25,562
Women without overt hypertension, but in whom the early development of preeclampsia is suspected, are seen more frequently.

21
00:01:25,562 --> 00:01:34,650
Heightened surveillance permits recognition of ominous changes in blood pressure, critical laboratory findings, and clinical signs and symptoms.

22
00:01:34,650 --> 00:01:43,850
Outpatient surveillance continues unless overt hypertension, proteinuria, headache, visual changes, or epigastric pain supervene.

23
00:01:43,850 --> 00:02:01,962
At Parkland Hospital, women with new-onset overt hypertension—either diastolic pressures ≥90 mm Hg or systolic pressures ≥140 mm Hg—are admitted to exclude preeclampsia or to define its severity.

24
00:02:01,962 --> 00:02:04,350
# Evaluation.

25
00:02:04,350 --> 00:02:34,112
With hospitalization, a systematic evaluation begins: • Detailed examination, which is coupled with daily scrutiny for headache, visual changes, or epigastric pain • Daily weight measurement to identify rapid weight gain • Quantification of proteinuria or a urine protein: creatinine ratio • Blood pressure readings with an appropriate-size cuff every 4 hours, unless previously elevated, which would mandate more frequent readings • Measurements of serum creatinine and hepatic transaminase levels

26
00:02:34,112 --> 00:02:37,400
and a hemogram that includes a platelet count.

27
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:41,812
The frequency of testing is determined by hypertension severity.

28
00:02:41,812 --> 00:03:04,225
Although some recommend assessment of serum uric acid and lactate dehydrogenase levels and coagulation, their value has been questioned (Chescheir, 2019; CondeAgudelo, 2015) • Evaluation of fetal size and well-being and amnionic fluid volume • Reduced physical activity may have benefits, although evidence is not robust.

29
00:03:04,225 --> 00:03:25,075
Still investigational as a clinical tool, measurements of placental growth factor (PlGF) and soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase 1 (sF1t-1) levels will likely be available to help predict preeclampsia (Barton, 2020; Chappell, 2013; Zeisler, 2016).

30
00:03:25,075 --> 00:03:31,350
Chapter 40 (p. 694) describes their role in preeclampsia genesis.

31
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:38,875
In sum, evaluation goals are early identification of preeclampsia and then management until timely delivery.

32
00:03:38,875 --> 00:03:44,050
Complete abatement of all signs and symptoms is uncommon until after delivery.

33
00:03:44,050 --> 00:03:53,887
If severe preeclampsia is diagnosed using the criteria in Table 40-2 (p. 690), further management is subsequently described.

34
00:03:53,887 --> 00:03:56,900
# Consideration for Delivery.

35
00:03:56,900 --> 00:04:07,462
With gestational hypertension, its morbidity and management vary depending on hypertension severity, presence of preeclampsia, and gestational age of the fetus.

36
00:04:07,462 --> 00:04:25,112
The basic management objectives for any pregnancy complicated by preeclampsia are: (1) termination of pregnancy with the least possible trauma to mother and fetus, (2) birth of a healthy newborn that subsequently thrives, and (3) complete restoration of health to the mother.

37
00:04:25,112 --> 00:04:33,275
In many with preeclampsia, especially those at or near term, all three objectives are served equally well by induction of labor.

38
00:04:33,275 --> 00:04:37,912
Termination of pregnancy is the only known cure for preeclampsia.

39
00:04:37,912 --> 00:04:46,912
Headache, visual changes, or epigastric pain are indicative that convulsions may be imminent, and oliguria is another ominous sign.

40
00:04:46,912 --> 00:04:54,700
Severe preeclampsia almost always demands anticonvulsant and antihypertensive therapy, followed by delivery.

41
00:04:54,700 --> 00:04:56,187
Treatment

42
00:04:56,356 --> 00:04:59,018
for eclampsia is identical.

43
00:04:58,968 --> 00:05:09,493
Prime objectives are to forestall convulsions, control blood pressure to prevent intracranial hemorrhage and serious damage to other organs, and deliver a healthy newborn.

44
00:05:09,493 --> 00:05:13,181
This is true even when the cervix is unfavorable.

45
00:05:13,181 --> 00:05:18,443
Labor induction is carried out, usually with preinduction cervical ripening .

46
00:05:18,443 --> 00:05:31,556
Concerns stemming from an unfavorable cervix, a perceived sense of urgency because of preeclampsia severity, and a need to coordinate neonatal intensive care have led some to advocate for cesarean delivery.

47
00:05:31,556 --> 00:05:46,406
In an earlier study from Parkland Hospital, Alexander and colleagues (1999) reviewed 278 singleton liveborn neonates weighing 750 to 1500 g delivered of women with severe preeclampsia.

48
00:05:46,406 --> 00:05:53,718
In half of the women, labor was induced, and induction was successful in accomplishing vaginal delivery in a third.

49
00:05:53,718 --> 00:05:57,981
Similar data were reported from the Consortium on Safe Labor .

50
00:05:57,981 --> 00:06:06,606
In this study, half of 914 women with severe preeclampsia underwent induction, and half of these were delivered vaginally.

51
00:06:06,606 --> 00:06:13,681
Others have reported similar observations (Alanis, 2008; Roland, 2017).

52
00:06:13,681 --> 00:06:20,981
For these reasons, we attempt labor induction and reserve cesarean delivery for other obstetrical indications.

53
00:06:20,981 --> 00:06:27,418
With preeclampsia without severe features, optimal delivery timing has not been widely studied.

54
00:06:27,418 --> 00:06:35,831
A randomized trial of 756 women with mild preeclampsia supported delivery after 37 weeks’ gestation .

55
00:06:35,831 --> 00:06:43,143
This practice is also supported by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a).

56
00:06:43,143 --> 00:06:50,456
At Parkland Hospital, we deliver women with preeclampsia without severe features at 38 weeks’ gestation.

57
00:06:50,456 --> 00:06:59,556
With a preterm fetus, the tendency is to delay delivery to help reduce the risk of neonatal death or serious morbidity from prematurity.

58
00:06:59,556 --> 00:07:03,781
Such a policy certainly is justified in milder cases.

59
00:07:03,781 --> 00:07:10,531
Assessments of fetal well-being and placental function are performed, especially when the fetus is immature.

60
00:07:10,531 --> 00:07:20,931
To assess these, most recommend frequent performance of nonstress testing or biophysical profiles, which are described in Chapter 20 (p. 392).

61
00:07:20,931 --> 00:07:36,943
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021a,b) recommends consideration for antenatal surveillance twice weekly for those with gestational hypertension and nonsevere features and daily testing for those with severe features.

62
00:07:36,943 --> 00:07:49,156
With late-preterm fetuses, that is, those between 34 and 36 weeks’ gestation, the decision to deliver is less clear (Barton, 2011; Langenveld, 2011).

63
00:07:49,156 --> 00:07:59,468
The Dutch HYPITAT-II study randomly assigned women with nonsevere hypertension between 34 and 37 weeks to immediate delivery or to expectant management .

64
00:07:59,468 --> 00:08:06,918
Immediate delivery reduced the risks for adverse maternal outcomes—1.1 versus 3.1 percent.

65
00:08:06,918 --> 00:08:15,043
However, it increased the risk for neonatal respiratory distress syndrome— 5.7 versus 1.7 percent.

66
00:08:15,043 --> 00:08:26,931
In a more recent study, 901 women at 34 to <37 weeks’ gestation with nonsevere preeclampsia were randomly assigned to early delivery or expectant management .

67
00:08:26,931 --> 00:08:34,368
A third of each group self-stratified into intervals of 34, 35, and 36 weeks’ gestation.

68
00:08:34,368 --> 00:08:45,093
For women in the immediate delivery group, rates of the primary maternal outcomes, which were features of severe preeclampsia, were significantly lower than in the expectant group.

69
00:08:45,093 --> 00:08:55,218
Conversely, rates of adverse perinatal outcomes, which were perinatal deaths or neonatal unit admission, were significantly greater in the immediate delivery group.

70
00:08:55,218 --> 00:09:02,568
Similar findings were reported by other (Bernardes, 2019; Chatzakis, 2021).

71
00:09:02,568 --> 00:09:11,406
Last, the PEACOCK study found that angiogenic biomarkers did not help determine the need for delivery in latepreterm preeclampsia .

72
00:09:11,406 --> 00:09:18,556
At Parkland Hospital, we favor an active management approach given the maternal risks of expectant management.

73
00:09:18,556 --> 00:09:25,193
However, if preeclampsia is nonsevere, we routinely induce after 38 completed weeks.

74
00:09:25,193 --> 00:09:28,493
# Inpatient or Outpatient Care.

75
00:09:28,493 --> 00:09:36,931
For women with mild to moderate stable hypertension, whether or not preeclampsia has been confirmed, monitoring is continued.

76
00:09:36,931 --> 00:09:44,143
During surveillance, reduced physical activity throughout much of the day, at least intuitively, seems reasonable.

77
00:09:44,143 --> 00:09:46,243
Complete bed rest

78
00:09:46,396 --> 00:09:57,096
was not recommended in the prior consensus work of the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists’ Task Force on Hypertension in Pregnancy (2013).

79
00:09:57,046 --> 00:10:07,271
Also, the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2020b) suggests that activity restriction not be prescribed for women with hypertensive disorders.

80
00:10:07,271 --> 00:10:14,483
Complete bed rest is pragmatically unachievable because of the severe restrictions it places on otherwise well women.

81
00:10:14,483 --> 00:10:22,608
It likely also predisposes to thromboembolism (Knight, 2007; McCarty-Singleton, 2014).

82
00:10:22,608 --> 00:10:29,646
To reduce activity, several studies have addressed the benefits of inpatient care and outpatient management.

83
00:10:29,646 --> 00:10:36,233
The concept of prolonged hospitalization for women with hypertension arose during the 1970s.

84
00:10:36,233 --> 00:10:45,621
At Parkland Hospital, an inpatient antepartum unit was established in 1973 by Dr. Peggy Whalley to provide care for such women.

85
00:10:45,621 --> 00:10:54,621
Initial results from this unit were reported by Hauth (1976) and Gilstrap (1978) and their coworkers.

86
00:10:54,621 --> 00:11:02,046
Most hospitalized women have a beneficial response characterized by amelioration or improvement of hypertension.

87
00:11:02,046 --> 00:11:08,808
These women are not “cured,” and nearly 90 percent have recurrent hypertension before or during labor.

88
00:11:08,808 --> 00:11:18,621
By 2020, more than 10,000 nulliparas with mild to moderate, early-onset hypertension during pregnancy had been managed successfully in this unit.

89
00:11:18,621 --> 00:11:27,971
This relatively simple unit requires modest nursing care, no drugs other than iron and folate supplements, and few essential laboratory tests.

90
00:11:27,971 --> 00:11:36,446
Provider costs—not charges—for this care are minimal compared with the cost of neonatal intensive care for a preterm neonate.

91
00:11:36,446 --> 00:11:41,021
Importantly, thromboembolic disease has been rare in these women.

92
00:11:41,021 --> 00:11:52,258
Many clinicians believe that further hospitalization is not warranted if hypertension abates within a few days, and this has emboldened third-party payers to deny inpatient reimbursement.

93
00:11:52,258 --> 00:11:57,733
Consequently, many women with mild to moderate hypertension are managed at home.

94
00:11:57,733 --> 00:12:04,171
For women in vulnerable sociodemographic home situations, this strategy may not be feasible.

95
00:12:04,171 --> 00:12:12,808
In other groups, outpatient management may continue as long as preeclampsia syndrome does not worsen and fetal jeopardy is not suspected.

96
00:12:12,808 --> 00:12:18,346
Decreased activity is recommended, and women are encouraged to report symptoms.

97
00:12:18,346 --> 00:12:25,496
Home blood pressure and urine protein monitoring or frequent evaluations by a visiting nurse may be beneficial.

98
00:12:25,496 --> 00:12:34,908
To assess this approach, 1182 nulliparas with mild gestational hypertension—20 percent had proteinuria—were managed with home care .

99
00:12:34,908 --> 00:12:42,708
Their mean gestational ages were 32 to 33 weeks at enrollment and 36 to 37 weeks at delivery.

100
00:12:42,708 --> 00:12:56,396
Severe preeclampsia developed in approximately 20 percent, two women had eclampsia, and 3 percent developed hemolysis, elevated liver enzyme levels, and low platelet count (HELLP) syndrome.

101
00:12:56,396 --> 00:12:59,471
Perinatal outcomes were generally good.

102
00:12:59,471 --> 00:13:08,496
In approximately 20 percent, fetal growth was restricted, and the perinatal mortality rate was 4.2 deaths in 1000 births.

103
00:13:08,496 --> 00:13:11,033
Table 41-1.

104
00:13:11,033 --> 00:13:16,471
presents two studies that compare continued hospitalization and outpatient care.

105
00:13:16,471 --> 00:13:28,521
In the first trial, after hospital evaluation, 218 women with mild gestational nonproteinuric hypertension were randomly assigned to continued hospitalization versus home care .

106
00:13:28,521 --> 00:13:37,971
The mean hospitalization duration was 22.2 days for women with inpatient management compared with only 6.5 days in the home care group.

107
00:13:37,971 --> 00:13:47,746
Severe hypertension and preterm delivery before 34 and before 37 weeks’ gestation were significantly increased twofold in the outpatient group.

108
00:13:47,746 --> 00:13:52,421
Despite this, maternal and newborn outcomes were otherwise similar.

109
00:13:52,421 --> 00:13:54,958
TABLE 41-1.

110
00:13:54,958 --> 00:14:07,033
Randomized Clinical Trials Comparing Hospitalization Versus Routine Care for Women with Mild Gestational Hypertension or Preeclampsia 1 Another approach, popular in Europe, is day care.

111
00:14:07,033 --> 00:14:15,621
In the second trial , 374 women with gestational hypertension were randomized to either day care or inpatient care .

112
00:14:15,621 --> 00:14:23,746
Almost 95 percent had mild to moderate hypertension and 86 women had ≥1+ proteinuria.

113
00:14:23,746 --> 00:14:30,308
There were no perinatal deaths, and none of the women developed eclampsia or HELLP syndrome.

114
00:14:30,308 --> 00:14:33,596
Costs for either scheme were not significantly

115
00:14:33,748 --> 00:14:37,685
different, and general satisfaction favored day care.

116
00:14:37,635 --> 00:14:47,985
In sum, either inpatient or close outpatient management is appropriate for a woman with mild de novo hypertension, including those with nonsevere preeclampsia.

117
00:14:47,985 --> 00:14:53,610
Keys to success are close surveillance and patients with home support and access to care.

118
00:14:53,610 --> 00:14:57,898
# Antihypertensive Therapy for Mild to Moderate.

119
00:14:57,898 --> 00:15:08,835
Hypertension Antihypertensive drugs have been evaluated to prolong pregnancy or modify perinatal outcomes in pregnancies complicated by various hypertensive disorders.

120
00:15:08,835 --> 00:15:16,885
Treatment for women with chronic hypertension complicating pregnancy is discussed in Chapter 53 (p. 949).

121
00:15:16,885 --> 00:15:21,360
Drug treatment for early mild preeclampsia has been disappointing .

122
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:31,010
Sibai and associates (1987a) reported that women given labetalol had significantly lower mean blood pressures than those given a placebo.

123
00:15:31,010 --> 00:15:38,773
However, mean pregnancy prolongation, gestational age at delivery, and birthweights did not differ between groups.

124
00:15:38,773 --> 00:15:45,160
The cesarean delivery rate and the number of newborns admitted to special-care nurseries also were similar.

125
00:15:45,160 --> 00:15:53,210
The frequency of growth-restricted neonates was doubled in women given labetalol compared with placebo—19 versus 9 percent.

126
00:15:53,210 --> 00:15:55,760
Table 41-2.

127
00:15:55,760 --> 00:16:09,823
Randomized Placebo-Controlled Trials of Antihypertensive Therapy for Early Mild Gestational Hypertension The three other studies listed in Table 41-2 compare labetalol or a calcium-channel blocker drugs against placebo.

128
00:16:09,823 --> 00:16:17,785
Except for fewer episodes of severe hypertension, none of these studies showed any benefits from antihypertensive treatment .

129
00:16:17,785 --> 00:16:29,385
One review of 49 randomized trials of antihypertensive therapy for mild to moderate gestational hypertension compared with either no treatment or placebo reached similar conclusions .

130
00:16:29,385 --> 00:16:34,135
# Expectant Management of Preterm Severe Preeclampsia.

131
00:16:34,135 --> 00:16:40,735
Up through the early 1990s, women with severe preeclampsia were usually immediately delivered.

132
00:16:40,735 --> 00:16:47,298
Subsequently, however, another approach for women with preterm severe preeclampsia was studied.

133
00:16:47,298 --> 00:16:53,885
The aim of “expectant” management was to improve neonatal outcome without compromising maternal safety.

134
00:16:53,885 --> 00:17:01,598
This approach always includes careful daily—and usually more frequent—inpatient monitoring of the mother and her fetus.

135
00:17:01,598 --> 00:17:11,398
Theoretically, antihypertensive therapy has potential application when severe preeclampsia develops before intact neonatal survival is expected.

136
00:17:11,398 --> 00:17:15,960
Such management has been controversial and is potentially dangerous .

137
00:17:15,960 --> 00:17:29,235
In one of the first studies, Sibai and the Memphis group (1985) attempted to prolong pregnancy because of fetal immaturity in 60 women with severe preeclampsia between 18 and 27 weeks.

138
00:17:29,235 --> 00:17:31,748
The results were disastrous.

139
00:17:31,748 --> 00:17:35,698
The perinatal mortality rate was 87 percent.

140
00:17:35,698 --> 00:17:50,923
Although no mothers died, 13 suffered placental abruption, 10 had eclampsia, three developed renal failure, two had hypertensive encephalopathy, one had an intracerebral hemorrhage, and another had a ruptured hepatic hematoma.

141
00:17:50,923 --> 00:18:06,073
Because of their early study, the Memphis group redefined criteria and performed a randomized trial of aggressive versus expectant management for 95 women who had severe preeclampsia but with more advanced gestations of 28 to 32 weeks .

142
00:18:06,073 --> 00:18:10,423
Women with HELLP syndrome were excluded from this trial.

143
00:18:10,423 --> 00:18:18,735
Aggressive management included glucocorticoid administration for fetal lung maturation followed by delivery in 48 hours.

144
00:18:18,735 --> 00:18:26,560
Expectantly managed women were observed at bed rest and given either labetalol or nifedipine orally for severe hypertension.

145
00:18:26,560 --> 00:18:33,598
In this study, pregnancy was prolonged for a mean of 15.4 days in the expectant management group.

146
00:18:33,598 --> 00:18:38,198
An overall improvement in neonatal outcomes also was reported.

147
00:18:38,198 --> 00:18:51,735
In women expectantly managed at 23 to 34 weeks’ gestation, serious complications have included placental abruption, HELLP syndrome, pulmonary edema, renal failure, and eclampsia .

148
00:18:51,735 --> 00:18:57,373
Moreover, perinatal mortality rates averaged 90 per 1000 births.

149
00:18:57,373 --> 00:19:06,885
Fetal-growth restriction was common, and in studies from The Netherlands, it was an astounding 94 percent (Ganzevoort, 2005a,b).

150
00:19:06,885 --> 00:19:15,048
Perinatal mortality rates were disproportionately high in these growth-restricted neonates (Haddad, 2007; Shear,

151
00:19:15,196 --> 00:19:19,021
2005). Table 41-3.

152
00:19:18,971 --> 00:19:37,346
Maternal and Perinatal Outcomes with Expectant Management of Severe Preeclampsia from 23 to 34 Weeks Following these experiences, expectant management became more commonly practiced, with the caveat that women with HELLP syndrome or growth-restricted fetuses were usually excluded.

153
00:19:37,346 --> 00:19:48,733
More recently, a prospective comparative study reported that expectant management of women with “stable” HELLP syndrome at <34 weeks may benefit mother and fetus .

154
00:19:48,733 --> 00:20:01,458
The MEXPRE Latin Study was a multicenter trial that randomly assigned 267 women with severe preeclampsia at 28 to 32 weeks’ gestation to prompt delivery or to expectant management .

155
00:20:01,458 --> 00:20:11,833
The perinatal mortality rate approximated 90 per 1000 in each group, and the composite neonatal morbidity outcome was not improved with expectant management.

156
00:20:11,833 --> 00:20:24,608
Conversely, fetal-growth restriction—22 versus 9 percent—and placental abruption—7.6 versus 1.5 percent—were significantly higher in the group managed expectantly.

157
00:20:24,608 --> 00:20:37,683
Churchill and colleagues (2018) performed a Cochrane Database review of six randomized trials of interventionist versus expectant care for severe preeclampsia between 24 and 34 weeks.

158
00:20:37,683 --> 00:20:46,671
Their review had findings similar to those in Table 41-3 and suggested that expectant management was beneficial to perinatal outcome.

159
00:20:46,671 --> 00:20:51,996
However, data were insufficient to establish conclusions on maternal health.

160
00:20:51,996 --> 00:20:56,508
# Expectant Management of Midtrimester Preeclampsia.

161
00:20:56,508 --> 00:21:04,321
Several small studies have focused on expectant management of severe preeclampsia syndrome before 28 weeks’ gestation.

162
00:21:04,321 --> 00:21:15,158
In one review of eight such studies, maternal complications were common among nearly 200 women with severe preeclampsia with an onset <26 completed weeks .

163
00:21:15,158 --> 00:21:23,683
Because no neonates survived when delivered before 23 weeks, most experts recommend pregnancy termination in these cases.

164
00:21:23,683 --> 00:21:29,396
For women with slightly more advanced pregnancies, however, the decision is less clear.

165
00:21:29,396 --> 00:21:39,008
For example, at 23 weeks’ gestation, the perinatal survival rate was 18 percent, but long-term perinatal morbidity is yet unknown.

166
00:21:39,008 --> 00:21:50,133
For women with pregnancies at 24 to 26 weeks, perinatal survival approaches 60 percent, and it averages almost 90 percent for those at 26 weeks.

167
00:21:50,133 --> 00:21:59,296
As shown in Table 41-4, maternal complications—especially HELLP syndrome—were commonplace and one mother died.

168
00:21:59,296 --> 00:22:02,783
Perinatal mortality exceeded 50 percent.

169
00:22:02,783 --> 00:22:15,008
At this time, no comparative studies attest to perinatal benefits of such expectant treatment versus early delivery in the face of serious maternal complications, which approach rates of 50 percent.

170
00:22:15,008 --> 00:22:17,583
Table 41-4.

171
00:22:17,583 --> 00:22:33,083
Pregnancy Outcomes with Expectant Management of Women with Severe Midtrimester Preeclampsiaa Corticosteroids for Lung Maturation To enhance fetal lung maturation, glucocorticoids have been administered to women with severe hypertension who are remote from term.

172
00:22:33,083 --> 00:22:42,358
Treatment does not seem to worsen maternal hypertension, and a lower incidence of respiratory distress and improved fetal survival rates have been cited.

173
00:22:42,358 --> 00:22:50,708
That said, only one randomized trial has evaluated corticosteroids given to hypertensive women for fetal lung maturation.

174
00:22:50,708 --> 00:23:02,571
This trial included 218 women who had severe preeclampsia between 26 and 34 weeks’ gestation and who were randomly assigned to betamethasone or placebo administration .

175
00:23:02,571 --> 00:23:13,946
Rates of neonatal complications that included respiratory distress, intraventricular hemorrhage, and death were reduced significantly when betamethasone was given compared with placebo.

176
00:23:13,946 --> 00:23:19,846
On the heavily weighted negative side, there were two maternal deaths and 18 stillbirths.

177
00:23:19,846 --> 00:23:31,183
We add these findings to buttress our unenthusiastic acceptance of attempts to prolong gestation in many of these women (Alexander, 2015; Bloom, 2003).

178
00:23:31,183 --> 00:23:47,146
Corticosteroids to Ameliorate HELLP Syndrome Several observational studies have indicated that corticosteroid therapy would ameliorate facets of the HELLP syndrome (Añez-Aguayo, 2018; Martin, 2016).

179
00:23:47,146 --> 00:23:58,908
Subsequently, at least three randomized trials aimed to evaluate the benefits of glucocorticoids given to improve the laboratory abnormalities associated with HELLP syndrome.

180
00:23:58,908 --> 00:24:00,946
Fonseca and

181
00:24:01,108 --> 00:24:11,295
associates (2005) randomly assigned 132 women with HELLP syndrome to either dexamethasone or placebo administration.

182
00:24:11,245 --> 00:24:26,895
Outcomes assessed included hospitalization length, recovery time of abnormal laboratory test results, resolution of clinical parameters, and complications that included acute renal failure, pulmonary edema, eclampsia, and death.

183
00:24:26,895 --> 00:24:30,895
None of these was significantly different between the two groups.

184
00:24:30,895 --> 00:24:39,158
In another study, 105 postpartum women with HELLP syndrome were assigned to dexamethasone or placebo treatment.

185
00:24:39,158 --> 00:24:44,520
Katz and colleagues (2008) found no advantage to dexamethasone .

186
00:24:44,520 --> 00:24:55,633
In the third study, preeclamptic women were given either placebo or methylprednisolone if their platelet count was between 50,000 and 150,000/μL .

187
00:24:55,633 --> 00:25:00,070
Again, no benefits were gained from corticosteroid therapy.

188
00:25:00,070 --> 00:25:09,020
A Bolivian study did show benefits from corticosteroid therapy, however, it was not randomized (Añez-Aguayo, 2018).

189
00:25:09,020 --> 00:25:18,920
Because of these findings, the 2013 Task Force does not recommend corticosteroid treatment to improve thrombocytopenia with HELLP syndrome.

190
00:25:18,920 --> 00:25:21,520
Figure 41-2.

191
00:25:21,520 --> 00:25:27,220
Clinical management algorithm for severe preeclampsia at <34 weeks.

192
00:25:27,220 --> 00:25:41,008
HELLP = hemolysis, elevated liver enzyme levels, low platelet count; L & D = labor and delivery; MgS04= magnesium sulfate; UOP = urine output.

193
00:25:41,008 --> 00:25:44,583
# Expectant Management Recommendations.

194
00:25:44,583 --> 00:25:56,970
Taken in toto, these studies do not show overwhelming benefits of expectant management of severe preeclampsia in women with gestations from 24 to 32 weeks compared with maternal risks.

195
00:25:56,970 --> 00:26:10,395
Despite these caveats, the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2011) has determined that such management is a reasonable alternative in selected women with severe preeclampsia before 34 weeks .

196
00:26:10,395 --> 00:26:15,308
The Task Force (2013) supports this recommendation.

197
00:26:15,308 --> 00:26:28,420
As shown in Table 41-5, such management calls for inpatient maternal and fetal surveillance with delivery prompted by evidence for worsening severe preeclampsia or maternal or fetal compromise.

198
00:26:28,420 --> 00:26:37,058
Although attempts are made for vaginal delivery in most cases, the likelihood of cesarean delivery rises with decreasing gestational age.

199
00:26:37,058 --> 00:26:39,645
Figure 41-1.

200
00:26:39,645 --> 00:26:51,495
Recovery times for platelet counts and serum aspartate transaminase (AST) levels in women with HELLP syndrome assigned to receive treatment with dexamethasone or placebo.

201
00:26:51,495 --> 00:26:54,195
Table 41-5.

202
00:26:54,195 --> 00:27:01,833
Indications for Delivery in Women <34 Weeks’ Gestation Managed Expectantly Our view is more conservative.

203
00:27:01,833 --> 00:27:08,958
Undoubtedly, the overriding reason to terminate pregnancies with severe preeclampsia is maternal safety.

204
00:27:08,958 --> 00:27:17,733
Indeed, it seems obvious that a delay to prolong gestation in women with severe preeclampsia may have serious maternal consequences.

205
00:27:17,733 --> 00:27:29,870
These observations are even more pertinent when considered with the absence of convincing evidence that perinatal outcomes are markedly improved by the average prolongation of pregnancy by approximately 1 week.

206
00:27:29,870 --> 00:27:37,058
If undertaken, the caveats that mandate delivery shown in Table 41-5 should be strictly heeded.

207
00:27:37,058 --> 00:27:40,020
# Experimental Therapies.

208
00:27:40,020 --> 00:27:49,020
In preliminary studies, therapies have been used to lower serum levels of antiangiogenic factors in hopes to mitigate their adverse actions.

209
00:27:49,020 --> 00:27:58,383
Some of these include therapeutic apheresis to lower sFlt-1 levels (Thadhani, 2016; Winkler, 2018).

210
00:27:58,383 --> 00:28:04,333
Another novel therapy uses RNA molecules to silence placental sFlt-1 .

211
00:28:04,333 --> 00:28:10,420
The proton-pump inhibitor esomeprazole was studied in women with early-onset preeclampsia .

212
00:28:10,420 --> 00:28:21,808
Sildenafil citrate, a phosphodiesterase inhibitor, has been provided to promote vasodilation (Trapani, 2016; Vigil-De Gracia, 2016).

213
00:28:21,808 --> 00:28:26,745
Other studies compared recombinant antithrombin infusion with placebo .

214
00:28:26,745 --> 00:28:30,633
In general, none of these therapies has shown promise.

215
00:28:30,633 --> 00:28:33,958
Severe Preeclampsia And Eclampsia.

216
00:28:33,958 --> 00:28:41,245
Classification of preeclampsia with severe features is summarized in Table 40-2 (p. 690).

217
00:28:41,245 --> 00:29:02,833
Briefly, features include systolic blood pressure ≥160 mm Hg or diastolic pressure ≥110 mm Hg; liver derangement with transaminitis; thrombocytopenia; renal insufficiency; pulmonary edema; new-onset headache; or visual changes.

218
00:29:02,833 --> 00:29:09,920
Eclampsia is defined by new-onset tonicclonic, focal, or multifocal seizures in the absence of other

219
00:29:10,084 --> 00:29:11,684
causes.

220
00:29:11,634 --> 00:29:13,909
# Eclampsia.

221
00:29:13,909 --> 00:29:19,121
The development of eclampsia appreciably raises the risk to mother and fetus .

222
00:29:19,121 --> 00:29:31,046
In an earlier report, Mattar and Sibai (2000) described outcomes in 399 consecutive women with eclampsia from 1977 through 1998.

223
00:29:31,046 --> 00:29:49,996
Major maternal complications included placental abruption— 10 percent, neurological deficits—7 percent, aspiration pneumonia—7 percent, pulmonary edema—5 percent, cardiopulmonary arrest—4 percent, and acute kidney injury (AKI)—4 percent.

224
00:29:49,996 --> 00:29:53,446
Moreover, 1 percent of these women died.

225
00:29:53,446 --> 00:30:09,284
Several subsequent reports similarly described excessive maternal morbidity and mortality rates with eclampsia that also included HELLP syndrome, pulmonary embolism, and stroke (Andersgaard, 2006; Knight, 2007).

226
00:30:09,284 --> 00:30:15,359
Almost without exception—but at times unnoticed— preeclampsia precedes convulsions.

227
00:30:15,359 --> 00:30:21,959
Eclampsia is most common in the last trimester and becomes increasingly frequent as term approaches.

228
00:30:21,959 --> 00:30:27,259
Postpartum, the incidence of eclampsia has declined during the past decade.

229
00:30:27,259 --> 00:30:37,309
Improved access to prenatal care, earlier detection of antepartum preeclampsia, and prophylactic use of magnesium sulfate are explanations .

230
00:30:37,309 --> 00:30:48,971
Other diagnoses should be considered in women with convulsions more than 48 hours postpartum or in women with focal neurological deficits, prolonged coma, or atypical eclampsia.

231
00:30:48,971 --> 00:30:52,371
# Clinical Findings with Eclampsia.

232
00:30:52,371 --> 00:30:58,446
Eclamptic seizures may be violent, and the woman must be protected, especially her airway.

233
00:30:58,446 --> 00:31:08,134
So forceful are the muscular movements that the woman may throw herself out of her bed, and if not protected, her tongue is bitten by the violent action of the jaws .

234
00:31:08,134 --> 00:31:14,796
This phase, in which the muscles alternately contract and relax, may last approximately a minute.

235
00:31:14,796 --> 00:31:17,434
Figure 41-3.

236
00:31:17,434 --> 00:31:22,021
Hematoma of tongue from laceration during an eclamptic convulsion.

237
00:31:22,021 --> 00:31:25,709
Thrombocytopenia may have contributed to the bleeding.

238
00:31:25,709 --> 00:31:32,646
Gradually, the muscular movements become smaller and less frequent, and finally the woman lies motionless.

239
00:31:32,646 --> 00:31:39,009
After a seizure, the woman is postictal, but in some, a coma of variable duration ensues.

240
00:31:39,009 --> 00:31:45,746
When the convulsions are infrequent, the woman usually recovers some degree of consciousness after each attack.

241
00:31:45,746 --> 00:31:50,434
As the woman arouses, a semiconscious combative state may ensue.

242
00:31:50,434 --> 00:31:56,521
In severe cases, coma persists from one convulsion to another, and death may result.

243
00:31:56,521 --> 00:32:02,334
Rarely, a single convulsion may be followed by coma from which the woman may never emerge.

244
00:32:02,334 --> 00:32:07,721
As a rule, however, death does not occur until after frequent convulsions.

245
00:32:07,721 --> 00:32:19,584
Last, and also rarely, convulsions continue unabated—status epilepticus—and require deep sedation and even general anesthesia to obviate anoxic encephalopathy.

246
00:32:19,584 --> 00:32:31,059
The respiratory rate after an eclamptic convulsion usually rises and may reach 50 or more per minute in response to hypercarbia, lactic acidemia, and transient hypoxia.

247
00:32:31,059 --> 00:32:34,709
Cyanosis may be observed in severe cases.

248
00:32:34,709 --> 00:32:40,234
High fever is a grave sign as it likely emanates from cerebrovascular hemorrhage.

249
00:32:40,234 --> 00:32:47,284
Proteinuria is usually present, and a fourth of women with severe preeclampsia have some degree of AKI .

250
00:32:47,284 --> 00:32:52,884
Urine output may be diminished appreciably, and occasionally anuria develops.

251
00:32:52,884 --> 00:32:57,659
Hemoglobinuria may be seen, but hemoglobinemia is rare.

252
00:32:57,659 --> 00:33:00,059
Edema may be pronounced .

253
00:33:00,059 --> 00:33:02,771
Figure 41-4.

254
00:33:02,771 --> 00:33:07,559
Severe edema in a young nullipara with antepartum preeclampsia.

255
00:33:07,559 --> 00:33:12,684
Fingerprint indentations from clinical assessment can be seen on both shins.

256
00:33:12,684 --> 00:33:16,521
Rings at the ankles formed from elastic sock bands.

257
00:33:16,521 --> 00:33:20,146
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Nidhi Shah.

258
00:33:20,146 --> 00:33:27,659
With severe preeclampsia, urinary output rises after delivery and is usually an early sign of improvement.

259
00:33:27,659 --> 00:33:32,884
With renal dysfunction, serum creatinine levels are serially monitored.

260
00:33:32,884 --> 00:33:37,784
Proteinuria and edema ordinarily disappear within a week postpartum.

261
00:33:37,784 --> 00:33:44,046
In most cases, blood pressure returns to normal within a few days to 2 weeks after delivery .

262
00:33:44,046 --> 00:33:51,771
As subsequently discussed, persisting and severe hypertension likely predicts underlying chronic vascular disease.

263
00:33:51,771 --> 00:33:59,809
In antepartum eclampsia, labor may begin spontaneously shortly after convulsions ensue and may progress rapidly.

264
00:33:59,809 --> 00:34:05,171
If the seizures develop during labor, contractions may increase in frequency

265
00:34:05,332 --> 00:34:09,407
and intensity, and the duration of labor may be shortened.

266
00:34:09,357 --> 00:34:17,582
Because of maternal hypoxemia and lactic acidemia caused by convulsions, fetal bradycardia often follows a seizure .

267
00:34:17,582 --> 00:34:23,394
In our experiences, the fetal heart rate usually recovers within 2 to 10 minutes.

268
00:34:23,394 --> 00:34:30,669
If it persists more than 10 minutes, another cause of bradycardia, such as placental abruption, should be considered.

269
00:34:30,669 --> 00:34:33,394
Figure 41-5.

270
00:34:33,394 --> 00:34:39,632
Fetal heart rate tracing shows fetal bradycardia following an intrapartum eclamptic convulsion.

271
00:34:39,632 --> 00:34:46,744
Bradycardia resolved and beat—to—beat variability returned approximately 5 minutes following the seizure.

272
00:34:46,744 --> 00:34:52,607
Pulmonary edema may follow shortly after eclamptic convulsions or several hours later.

273
00:34:52,607 --> 00:34:57,519
This complication is further explored on page 724.

274
00:34:57,519 --> 00:35:04,519
Occasionally, sudden death occurs synchronously with an eclamptic seizure, or it follows shortly thereafter.

275
00:35:04,519 --> 00:35:12,494
Most often in these cases, a massive cerebral hemorrhage is the cause (Fig. 40-11, p. 700).

276
00:35:12,494 --> 00:35:16,044
Hemiplegia may result from sublethal hemorrhage.

277
00:35:16,044 --> 00:35:21,919
Cerebral hemorrhages are more likely in older women with underlying chronic hypertension.

278
00:35:21,919 --> 00:35:31,757
For women with a neurological deficit after an eclamptic seizure, consideration is given for emergent cranial computed tomography (CT) scanning.

279
00:35:31,757 --> 00:35:39,332
Up to 5 percent of women with eclampsia have altered consciousness, including persistent coma, following a seizure.

280
00:35:39,332 --> 00:35:46,469
This may be due to extensive cerebral edema, and associated transtentorial herniation may cause death .

281
00:35:46,469 --> 00:35:52,569
In approximately 10 percent of eclamptic women, some degree of blindness follows a seizure.

282
00:35:52,569 --> 00:35:59,844
The causes of impaired vision which usually improves postpartum, are discussed in Chapter 40 (p. 701).

283
00:35:59,844 --> 00:36:05,244
Rarely, eclampsia is followed by psychosis, and the woman becomes violent.

284
00:36:05,244 --> 00:36:08,532
This may last for several days to 2 weeks.

285
00:36:08,532 --> 00:36:15,194
The prognosis for return to normal function is good, provided mental illness was not preexisting.

286
00:36:15,194 --> 00:36:22,444
It is presumed to be similar to postpartum psychosis discussed in Chapter 64 (p. 1148).

287
00:36:22,444 --> 00:36:30,032
Antipsychotic medications have been effective in the few cases of posteclampsia psychosis treated at Parkland Hospital.

288
00:36:30,032 --> 00:36:36,019
Generally, eclampsia is more likely to be diagnosed too frequently rather than overlooked.

289
00:36:36,019 --> 00:36:51,969
Epilepsy, encephalitis, meningitis, brain tumor, neurocysticercosis, amnionic fluid embolism, postdural puncture cephalalgia, and ruptured cerebral aneurysm during late pregnancy or in the puerperium may simulate eclampsia.

290
00:36:51,969 --> 00:36:59,607
Until other such causes are excluded, however, all pregnant women with convulsions should be considered to have eclampsia.

291
00:36:59,607 --> 00:37:06,857
As a pragmatic rule, loading with magnesium sulfate should be considered while alternate diagnoses are explored.

292
00:37:06,857 --> 00:37:10,469
Management Of Severe Preeclampsiaeclampsia.

293
00:37:10,469 --> 00:37:21,032
Most eclampsia regimens in the United States adhere to a similar philosophy: • Control or prevent convulsions using an intravenous loading dose of magnesium sulfate.

294
00:37:21,032 --> 00:37:43,207
This is followed by maintenance dosing, usually given intravenously • Provide intermittent antihypertensive medication to lower dangerously high blood pressure • Avoid diuretics unless pulmonary edema is obvious; limit intravenous fluid administration unless fluid loss is excessive; and avoid hyperosmotic agents • Deliver the fetus to resolve preeclampsia.

295
00:37:43,207 --> 00:37:46,044
# Magnesium Sulfate.

296
00:37:46,044 --> 00:37:53,607
This parenterally administered agent is an effective anticonvulsant and avoids producing central nervous system depression.

297
00:37:53,607 --> 00:38:00,369
It may be given intravenously by continuous infusion or intramuscularly by intermittent injection .

298
00:38:00,369 --> 00:38:05,357
A third option is intermittent, intravenous, 2-g injections .

299
00:38:05,357 --> 00:38:09,869
Dosages for severe preeclampsia mirror those for eclampsia.

300
00:38:09,869 --> 00:38:22,594
Because labor and delivery is a more likely time for seizures to develop, women with severe preeclampsia or eclampsia usually are given magnesium sulfate during labor and for 24 hours postpartum.

301
00:38:22,594 --> 00:38:28,969
In the United States, magnesium sulfate is almost universally administered intravenously.

302
00:38:28,969 --> 00:38:38,132
Of concern, magnesium sulfate solutions, although inexpensive to prepare, are not readily available in all parts of the developing world.

303
00:38:38,132 --> 00:38:43,382
Even if solutions are available, the technology to infuse them may not be.

304
00:38:43,382 --> 00:38:46,319
Thus, the drug can be administered

305
00:38:46,468 --> 00:38:58,380
intramuscularly, and this is as effective as intravenous infusion (Pritchard, 1955, 1975, 1984; Salinger, 2013).

306
00:38:58,330 --> 00:39:00,980
Table 41-6.

307
00:39:00,980 --> 00:39:09,505
Magnesium Sulfate Dosage Schedule for Severe Preeclampsia and Eclampsia Magnesium sulfate is not given to treat hypertension.

308
00:39:09,505 --> 00:39:16,168
Magnesium ion most likely exerts a specific anticonvulsant action on the cerebral cortex.

309
00:39:16,168 --> 00:39:21,655
Typically, the mother stops convulsing after the initial 4-g loading dose.

310
00:39:21,655 --> 00:39:27,818
By an hour or two, she regains consciousness sufficiently to be oriented to place and time.

311
00:39:27,818 --> 00:39:36,655
The magnesium sulfate dosage regimens presented in Table 41-6 usually result in increased plasma magnesium levels.

312
00:39:36,655 --> 00:39:43,855
Data from Brookfield and associates (2016) are illustrated in Figure 41-6.

313
00:39:43,855 --> 00:39:51,818
When magnesium sulfate is given to arrest an eclamptic seizure, up to 15 percent of women will have a subsequent convulsion.

314
00:39:51,818 --> 00:39:59,993
If so, an additional 2-g dose of magnesium sulfate in a 20-percent solution is slowly administered intravenously.

315
00:39:59,993 --> 00:40:07,543
In a small woman, this additional 2-g dose may be used once, but it can be given twice if needed in a larger woman.

316
00:40:07,543 --> 00:40:18,318
In only 5 of 245 women with eclampsia at Parkland Hospital was it necessary to use alternative supplementary anticonvulsant medication to control seizures .

317
00:40:18,318 --> 00:40:26,130
For these, a small dose of a short-acting benzodiazepine such as midazolam or lorazepam is given intravenously.

318
00:40:26,130 --> 00:40:32,618
Their prolonged use is avoided because of an associated higher mortality rate from aspiration pneumonia .

319
00:40:32,618 --> 00:40:35,368
Figure 41-6.

320
00:40:35,368 --> 00:40:45,605
Serum magnesium concentration in normotensive and preeclamptic women following a 4-g loading dose of magnesium sulfate and 2 g/h infusion.

321
00:40:45,605 --> 00:40:52,405
Maintenance magnesium sulfate therapy has traditionally been continued for 24 hours after delivery.

322
00:40:52,405 --> 00:41:00,868
For eclampsia that develops postpartum, magnesium sulfate is administered for 24 hours after the onset of convulsions.

323
00:41:00,868 --> 00:41:13,480
A few investigators have truncated this therapy duration to 12 hours and reported no seizures (Anjum, 2016; Ehrenberg, 2006; Kashanian, 2016).

324
00:41:13,480 --> 00:41:23,780
Outcomes have also been reported when magnesium sulfate therapy was stopped after delivery (Ludmir, 2017; Vigil-De Gracia, 2018).

325
00:41:23,780 --> 00:41:32,418
These studies are relatively small, and the abbreviated magnesium sulfate regimens need further study before being routinely implemented.

326
00:41:32,418 --> 00:41:47,818
Pharmacology and Toxicology Parenterally administered magnesium is cleared almost totally by renal excretion, and magnesium clearance rate is approximately a third of glomerular filtration rate (GFR) determined by creatinine clearance.

327
00:41:47,818 --> 00:41:53,993
Magnesium intoxication is unusual when the GFR is normal or only slightly reduced.

328
00:41:53,993 --> 00:41:58,680
Adequate urine output usually correlates with preserved GFR.

329
00:41:58,680 --> 00:42:08,055
That said, magnesium excretion is not urine flow dependent, and urinary volume per unit time does not, per se, predict renal function.

330
00:42:08,055 --> 00:42:13,580
Thus, serum creatinine levels must be measured to confirm a decreased GFR.

331
00:42:13,580 --> 00:42:29,718
Eclamptic convulsions are almost always prevented or arrested by plasma magnesium levels maintained at 4 to 7 mEq/L, 4.8 to 8.4 mg/dL, or 2.0 to 3.5 mmol/L.

332
00:42:29,718 --> 00:42:37,968
However, one review of magnesium pharmacokinetics showed that most regimens result in much lower serum magnesium levels .

333
00:42:37,968 --> 00:42:42,855
This was especially true if only 1 g/hr was infused .

334
00:42:42,855 --> 00:42:47,755
Importantly, the obesity epidemic has affected these observations .

335
00:42:47,755 --> 00:42:56,405
Tudela and colleagues (2013) described our results from Parkland Hospital with magnesium administration to obese women.

336
00:42:56,405 --> 00:43:09,455
More than 60 percent of women who had a body mass index (BMI) >30 kg/m2 and who were receiving a 2-g/hr dose had subtherapeutic levels at 4 hours.

337
00:43:09,455 --> 00:43:16,243
Thus, most obese women would require 3 g/hr to maintain effective plasma levels.

338
00:43:16,243 --> 00:43:30,643
That said, most currently do not recommend routine magnesium level measurements (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020a; Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists, 2006).

339
00:43:30,643 --> 00:43:42,780
Patellar reflexes disappear when the plasma magnesium level reaches 10 mEq/L—approximately 12 mg/dL—presumably because of a curariform action.

340
00:43:42,780 --> 00:43:47,093
This sign serves to warn of impending magnesium toxicity.

341
00:43:47,093 --> 00:43:52,743
When plasma levels rise above 10 mEq/L, breathing weakens.

342
00:43:52,743 --> 00:43:59,868
At 12 mEq/L or higher levels, respiratory paralysis and respiratory arrest

343
00:44:00,028 --> 00:44:01,565
follow .

344
00:44:01,515 --> 00:44:13,678
Treating with calcium gluconate or calcium chloride, 1 g intravenously, coupled with magnesium sulfate discontinuation, usually reverses mild to moderate respiratory depression.

345
00:44:13,678 --> 00:44:19,115
One of these agents should be readily available whenever magnesium is being infused.

346
00:44:19,115 --> 00:44:27,790
Unfortunately, the effects of intravenously administered calcium may be short-lived if a steady-state toxic magnesium level has been reached.

347
00:44:27,790 --> 00:44:35,390
For severe respiratory depression and arrest, prompt tracheal intubation and mechanical ventilation are lifesaving.

348
00:44:35,390 --> 00:44:41,753
Direct toxic effects on the myocardium from high levels of magnesium sulfate are uncommon .

349
00:44:41,753 --> 00:44:52,815
Because magnesium is cleared almost exclusively by renal excretion, our described dosages of magnesium sulfate will become excessive if the GFR is substantially decreased.

350
00:44:52,815 --> 00:45:00,503
The initial 4- or 6-g loading dose of magnesium sulfate can be safely administered regardless of renal function.

351
00:45:00,503 --> 00:45:05,440
It is important to administer the standard loading dose and not to reduce it.

352
00:45:05,440 --> 00:45:11,990
A loading dose achieves the desired therapeutic level, and the infusion maintains the steady-state level.

353
00:45:11,990 --> 00:45:17,890
Thus, only the maintenance infusion rate should be altered for those with a diminished GFR.

354
00:45:17,890 --> 00:45:22,215
Renal function is estimated by measuring plasma creatinine.

355
00:45:22,215 --> 00:45:31,678
Whenever plasma creatinine levels are >1.0 mg/mL, serial serum magnesium levels are determined to guide the infusion rate .

356
00:45:31,678 --> 00:45:42,715
After a 4-g intravenous dose is administered during 15 minutes, mean arterial pressure falls slightly and is accompanied by a 13-percent rise in cardiac index .

357
00:45:42,715 --> 00:45:48,653
Thus, magnesium lowers systemic vascular resistance and mean arterial pressure.

358
00:45:48,653 --> 00:45:52,453
At the same time, cardiac output is increased.

359
00:45:52,453 --> 00:46:03,178
These findings are coincidental with transient nausea and flushing, and the cardiovascular effects persist for only 15 minutes despite continued magnesium infusion.

360
00:46:03,178 --> 00:46:10,290
Magnesium-induced vasodilation is weaker in placental vessels because of diminished calcium-channel activity .

361
00:46:10,290 --> 00:46:21,190
Thurnau and coworkers (1987) showed that magnesium therapy led to a small but significant rise in the cerebrospinal fluid’s total magnesium concentration.

362
00:46:21,190 --> 00:46:26,628
The magnitude was directly proportional to the corresponding serum concentration.

363
00:46:26,628 --> 00:46:32,728
Other Effects Magnesium has anticonvulsant and neuroprotective effects in animal models.

364
00:46:32,728 --> 00:46:59,490
Some proposed mechanisms of action include: (1) reduced presynaptic release of the neurotransmitter glutamate, (2) blockade of glutamatergic Nmethyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptors, (3) potentiation of adenosine action, (4) improved calcium buffering by mitochondria, and (5) blockage of calcium entry via voltage-gated channels (Arango, 2008; Wang, 2012).

365
00:46:59,490 --> 00:47:06,640
In the uterus, relatively high serum magnesium concentrations depress myometrial contractility.

366
00:47:06,640 --> 00:47:17,753
Inhibition of uterine contractility is magnesium dose dependent, and serum levels of at least 8 to 10 mEq/L are needed to inhibit uterine contractions .

367
00:47:17,753 --> 00:47:25,790
With the suggested magnesium sulfate regimens for seizure prevention, prolonged myometrial depression has not been observed.

368
00:47:25,790 --> 00:47:36,828
A transient decline in activity during and immediately after the initial intravenous loading dose can be seen (Leveno, 1998; Witlin, 1997).

369
00:47:36,828 --> 00:47:42,328
Blood loss at delivery is not increased by standard magnesium sulfate treatment .

370
00:47:42,328 --> 00:47:46,453
# Neuroprophylaxis—Prevention of Seizures.

371
00:47:46,453 --> 00:47:55,628
Several randomized trials have tested the efficacy of seizure prophylaxis for women with gestational hypertension, with or without proteinuria.

372
00:47:55,628 --> 00:48:01,715
In all, magnesium sulfate was superior to the comparator agent to prevent eclampsia.

373
00:48:01,715 --> 00:48:06,728
Four of the larger studies are summarized in Table 41-7.

374
00:48:06,728 --> 00:48:17,215
In the study from Parkland Hospital, magnesium sulfate therapy was superior to phenytoin to prevent seizures in women with gestational hypertension or preeclampsia .

375
00:48:17,215 --> 00:48:30,115
In another study, magnesium sulfate and nimodipine, which is a calcium-channel blocking drug with specific cerebral vasodilator activity, were compared in 1650 women with severe preeclampsia .

376
00:48:30,115 --> 00:48:38,840
The rate of eclampsia was more than threefold higher for women allocated to the nimodipine group—2.6 versus 0.8 percent.

377
00:48:38,840 --> 00:48:41,515
Table 41-7.

378
00:48:41,515 --> 00:48:45,428
Randomized Comparative Trials of Prophylaxis with

379
00:48:45,580 --> 00:49:00,505
Magnesium Sulfate and Placebo or Another Anticonvulsant in Women with Gestational Hypertension The largest comparative study was Magnesium Sulfate for Prevention of Eclampsia reported by the Magpie Trial Collaboration Group (2002).

380
00:49:00,505 --> 00:49:09,792
More than 10,000 women with severe preeclampsia from 33 countries were randomly allocated to treatment with magnesium sulfate or placebo.

381
00:49:09,792 --> 00:49:16,942
Women given magnesium had a 58-percent significantly lower risk of eclampsia than those given placebo.

382
00:49:16,942 --> 00:49:21,567
Maternal mortality and placental abruption rates also were decreased.

383
00:49:21,567 --> 00:49:25,967
Child behavior at 18 months did not differ between groups .

384
00:49:25,967 --> 00:49:28,942
Who Should Be Given Magnesium Sulfate?

385
00:49:28,942 --> 00:49:35,042
Magnesium will prevent proportionately more seizures in women with correspondingly worse disease.

386
00:49:35,042 --> 00:49:44,030
However, severity is difficult to quantify, and thus deciding which woman might benefit most from neuroprophylaxis is sometimes difficult.

387
00:49:44,030 --> 00:49:56,280
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) recommends that women with either eclampsia or severe preeclampsia should be given magnesium sulfate prophylaxis.

388
00:49:56,280 --> 00:50:00,942
Again, criteria that establish “severity” are not universal.

389
00:50:00,942 --> 00:50:09,917
Thus, the conundrum is whether women with “nonsevere” gestational hypertension or preeclampsia should receive magnesium neuroprophylaxis.

390
00:50:09,917 --> 00:50:20,130
We found that approximately 1 woman in 100 who has nonsevere preeclampsia but who is not given magnesium sulfate prophylaxis can be expected to have a seizure.

391
00:50:20,130 --> 00:50:29,642
A fourth of these women likely will require emergent cesarean delivery and be exposed to the attendant maternal and perinatal morbidity from general anesthesia.

392
00:50:29,642 --> 00:50:41,942
From this, the major question regarding management of nonsevere gestational hypertension remains whether it is acceptable to avoid unnecessary treatment of 99 women to risk eclampsia in one.

393
00:50:41,942 --> 00:50:50,192
The answer appears to be yes, as suggested by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a).

394
00:50:50,192 --> 00:51:04,342
At Parkland Hospital, our policy is to give magnesium neuroprophylaxis to women with preeclampsia with severe features, and conservatively to those with proteinuria hypertension (Table 40-1, p. 689).

395
00:51:04,342 --> 00:51:19,617
Fetal and Neonatal Effects Magnesium administered parenterally promptly crosses the placenta to achieve equilibrium in fetal serum and less so in amnionic fluid (GortzakUzan, 2005; Narasimhulu, 2017).

396
00:51:19,617 --> 00:51:27,555
Magnesium sulfate has small but significant effects on the fetal heart rate pattern and specifically on beat-to-beat variability .

397
00:51:27,555 --> 00:51:36,217
One study showed a lower baseline heart rate that was within the normal range; decreased variability; and fewer prolonged decelerations .

398
00:51:36,217 --> 00:51:41,142
Overall, maternal magnesium therapy appears safe for perinates .

399
00:51:41,142 --> 00:51:51,742
One study of more than 1500 exposed preterm neonates found no association between the need for neonatal resuscitation and cord blood magnesium levels .

400
00:51:51,742 --> 00:51:56,630
Still, a few neonatal adverse events are associated with its use.

401
00:51:56,630 --> 00:52:05,342
In a Parkland Hospital study of 6654 mostly term, exposed newborns, 6 percent had hypotonia .

402
00:52:05,342 --> 00:52:15,067
In addition, exposed neonates had lower 1- and 5-minute Apgar scores, a higher intubation rate, and more admissions to the special care nursery.

403
00:52:15,067 --> 00:52:20,530
Neonatal depression occurs only if hypermagnesemia at delivery is severe.

404
00:52:20,530 --> 00:52:29,280
Observational studies suggest a protective effect of magnesium against the development of cerebral palsy in very-low- birthweight newborns .

405
00:52:29,280 --> 00:52:33,880
These beneficial effects may extend to growth-restricted fetuses.

406
00:52:33,880 --> 00:52:43,617
Randomized trials have also assessed neuroprotective effects for preterm neonates, and findings are discussed in Chapter 45 (p. 803).

407
00:52:43,617 --> 00:52:51,080
One review expanded this possibility to include term newborns, but data were insufficient to draw conclusions .

408
00:52:51,080 --> 00:53:08,842
Maternal Safety and Efficacy The multinational Eclampsia Trial Collaborative Group study (1995) involved 1687 women with eclampsia randomly allocated to one of three different anticonvulsant regimens: magnesium sulfate, diazepam, or phenytoin .

409
00:53:08,842 --> 00:53:23,792
In aggregate, magnesium sulfate therapy was associated with a significantly lower incidence of recurrent seizures (9.7 percent) compared with women given phenytoin (28 percent) or diazepam (17 percent).

410
00:53:23,792 --> 00:53:27,017
Moreover, the aggregate maternal death

411
00:53:27,172 --> 00:53:35,572
rate of 3.2 percent with magnesium sulfate was significantly lower than that of 5.2 percent for the other two regimens.

412
00:53:35,522 --> 00:53:38,084
Table 41-8.

413
00:53:38,084 --> 00:53:48,584
Randomized Comparative Trials of Magnesium Sulfate versus Phenytoin and Diazepam to Prevent Recurrent Eclamptic Convulsions # Severe Hypertension Treatment.

414
00:53:48,584 --> 00:53:57,359
Dangerous hypertension can cause cerebrovascular hemorrhage and hypertensive encephalopathy, and it can trigger eclamptic convulsions.

415
00:53:57,359 --> 00:54:04,647
Other complications are placental abruption and congestive heart failure induced by elevated hypertensive afterload.

416
00:54:04,647 --> 00:54:18,184
Studies highlight the importance of treating systolic hypertension when blood pressures are >160 mm Hg (Judy, 2019; Martin, 2005, 2016).

417
00:54:18,184 --> 00:54:41,972
Because of these serious sequelae, the Working Group for the National High Blood Pressure Education Program (2000) and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) recommend treatment to lower systolic pressures to a level ≤160 mm Hg and diastolic pressures to ≤110 mm Hg.

418
00:54:41,972 --> 00:54:46,497
Lower diastolic pressures can compromise placental perfusion.

419
00:54:46,497 --> 00:55:00,284
From other observations, it seems likely that at least half of serious hemorrhagic strokes associated with preeclampsia are in women with chronic hypertension (Cunningham, 2005; Zofkie, 2018).

420
00:55:00,284 --> 00:55:11,022
Long-standing hypertension results in development of Charcot–Bouchard aneurysms in the deep penetrating arteries of the lenticulostriate branch of the middle cerebral arteries.

421
00:55:11,022 --> 00:55:19,234
These vessels supply the basal ganglia, putamen, thalamus, adjacent deep white matter, pons, and deep cerebellum.

422
00:55:19,234 --> 00:55:26,547
These unique aneurysmal weakenings predispose these small arteries to rupture during sudden hypertensive episodes.

423
00:55:26,547 --> 00:55:34,597
Several drugs are available to rapidly lower dangerously elevated blood pressure in women with pregnancy-associated hypertension.

424
00:55:34,597 --> 00:55:44,997
Hydralazine, labetalol, and nifedipine are recommended as first-line agents by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a).

425
00:55:44,997 --> 00:55:48,784
Evidence supports their value to decrease stroke risk .

426
00:55:48,784 --> 00:55:52,034
All three agents have equivalent efficacy.

427
00:55:52,034 --> 00:56:00,722
Comparative studies of hydralazine and labetalol show similar results (Mable, 1987; Umans, 2015).

428
00:56:00,722 --> 00:56:11,159
Hydralazine causes significantly more maternal tachycardia and palpitations, whereas labetalol more frequently leads to maternal hypotension and bradycardia.

429
00:56:11,159 --> 00:56:24,584
Randomized trials that compared nifedipine with labetalol found neither drug definitively superior, but nifedipine lowered blood pressure more quickly (Gainder, 2019; Zulfeen, 2019).

430
00:56:24,584 --> 00:56:30,134
Both drugs are associated with a reduced frequency of fetal heart rate accelerations .

431
00:56:30,134 --> 00:56:35,784
Last, a study comparing nifedipine and hydralazine showed similar efficacy .

432
00:56:35,784 --> 00:56:43,184
A few other generally available antihypertensive agents have been tested in clinical trials but are not widely used .

433
00:56:43,184 --> 00:56:54,359
These include verapamil, nitroglycerin, nitroprusside, ketanserin, nicardipine, and nimodipine (Belfort, 2003; Cornette, 2016).

434
00:56:54,359 --> 00:57:00,559
Experimental antihypertensive drugs may eventually be useful for preeclampsia treatment .

435
00:57:00,559 --> 00:57:06,897
Hydralazine This antihypertensive agent is administered intravenously or intramuscularly.

436
00:57:06,897 --> 00:57:12,397
An initial 5- to 10-mg dose can act as rapidly as 10 minutes.

437
00:57:12,397 --> 00:57:20,947
If needed, this is followed by 10-mg doses at 15- to 20-minute intervals until a satisfactory response is achieved .

438
00:57:20,947 --> 00:57:33,759
Although we will administer a third dose, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) recommends labetalol therapy if severe hypertension persists after the second dose.

439
00:57:33,759 --> 00:57:42,747
Another regimen continuously infuses hydralazine at a rate of 0.5 to 10 mg/hr. Table 41-9.

440
00:57:42,747 --> 00:57:53,034
Antihypertensive Agents for Urgent Control of Severe Hypertension For higher blood pressures, the tendency to give a larger initial dose of hydralazine should be avoided.

441
00:57:53,034 --> 00:57:59,272
The response to 5- to 10-mg doses cannot be predicted by hypertension severity.

442
00:57:59,272 --> 00:58:04,872
Thus, our protocol is to always administer 5 mg as the initial dose.

443
00:58:04,872 --> 00:58:10,759
An adverse response to exceeding this initial dose is shown in Figure 41-7.

444
00:58:10,759 --> 00:58:20,397
This woman had chronic hypertension complicated by severe superimposed preeclampsia, and hydralazine was injected more frequently than recommended.

445
00:58:20,397 --> 00:58:23,709
Her blood pressure in less than 1 hour dropped

446
00:58:23,860 --> 00:58:35,972
from 270/150 mm Hg to 110/80 mm Hg. Fetal heart rate decelerations characteristic of uteroplacental insufficiency became evident.

447
00:58:35,922 --> 00:58:41,935
Decelerations persisted until her blood pressure was raised with rapid crystalloid infusion.

448
00:58:41,935 --> 00:58:53,972
In some cases, this fetal response to diminished uterine perfusion may be confused with placental abruption and may result in unnecessary and potentially dangerous emergency cesarean delivery.

449
00:58:53,972 --> 00:58:56,660
Figure 41-7.

450
00:58:56,660 --> 00:59:02,122
Hydralazine was given at 5-minute intervals instead of 15-minute intervals.

451
00:59:02,122 --> 00:59:10,947
The mean arterial pressure dropped from 180 to 90 mm Hg within 1 hour and was associated with fetal bradycardia.

452
00:59:10,947 --> 00:59:18,647
Rapid crystalloid infusion raised the mean pressure to 115 mm Hg, and the fetus recovered.

453
00:59:18,647 --> 00:59:25,285
Labetalol This effective intravenous antihypertensive agent is an α- and nonselective βblocker.

454
00:59:25,285 --> 00:59:29,860
Some prefer its use to hydralazine because of fewer side effects.

455
00:59:29,860 --> 00:59:34,597
Importantly, labetalol is usually not given to asthmatic women.

456
00:59:34,597 --> 00:59:39,660
At Parkland Hospital, we give 10 mg intravenously initially.

457
00:59:39,660 --> 00:59:45,847
If blood pressure has not declined to suitable levels in 10 minutes, 20 mg is given.

458
00:59:45,847 --> 00:59:52,672
The next 10-minute incremental dose is 40 mg and is followed by another 40 mg if needed.

459
00:59:52,672 --> 00:59:58,235
If a salutary response is not achieved, an 80-mg dose is provided .

460
00:59:58,235 --> 01:00:07,935
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) recommends starting with a 10- to 20-mg intravenous bolus.

461
01:00:07,935 --> 01:00:13,972
If not effective, this is followed by 20 to 80 mg every 10 to 30 minutes.

462
01:00:13,972 --> 01:00:18,160
If hypertension persists, hydralazine is then given.

463
01:00:18,160 --> 01:00:26,822
Nifedipine This calcium-channel blocking agent is given initially as a 10- to 20-mg oral dose of immediate release medication.

464
01:00:26,822 --> 01:00:32,897
If necessary after 20 minutes, a 10- to 20-mg oral dose is repeated.

465
01:00:32,897 --> 01:00:37,372
With an unsatisfactory response, labetalol is provided.

466
01:00:37,372 --> 01:00:41,585
Nifedipine given sublingually is no longer recommended.

467
01:00:41,585 --> 01:00:46,572
This route is associated with dangerously rapid and extensive effects.

468
01:00:46,572 --> 01:00:51,935
Diuretics Potent loop diuretics can further compromise placental perfusion.

469
01:00:51,935 --> 01:01:02,110
Immediate effects include redistribution of intravascular volume, which most often is already reduced in severe preeclampsia (p. 724).

470
01:01:02,110 --> 01:01:11,135
Thus, before delivery, diuretics are not used to lower blood pressure (Zeeman, 2009; Zondervan, 1988).

471
01:01:11,135 --> 01:01:17,172
Furosemide or similar drugs are used before delivery solely to treat pulmonary edema.

472
01:01:17,172 --> 01:01:23,910
Discussed on page 725, they may have a role in treatment of postpartum hypertension.

473
01:01:23,910 --> 01:01:26,385
# Fluid Therapy.

474
01:01:26,385 --> 01:01:39,860
Crystalloid solution is administered routinely at a rate between 60 and 125 mL per hour, unless fluid loss is unusual from vomiting, diarrhea, diaphoresis, or excessive blood loss.

475
01:01:39,860 --> 01:01:43,622
Oliguria is common with severe preeclampsia.

476
01:01:43,622 --> 01:01:53,735
Coupled with the knowledge that maternal blood volume is likely constricted compared with that of normal pregnancy, it is tempting to administer intravenous fluids more vigorously.

477
01:01:53,735 --> 01:02:01,247
However, controlled, conservative fluid administration is preferred for the typical woman with severe preeclampsia.

478
01:02:01,247 --> 01:02:09,410
These gravidas already have excessive extracellular fluid that has inappropriately extravasated from the intravascular compartment.

479
01:02:09,410 --> 01:02:21,797
Infusion of large fluid volumes enhances the maldistribution and thereby elevates the risk of pulmonary and cerebral edema (Sciscioner, 2003; Zinaman, 1985).

480
01:02:21,797 --> 01:02:31,197
Thus, for preeclamptic women with anuria, small incremental boluses can be given to maintain urine output above 30 mL/hr.

481
01:02:31,197 --> 01:02:40,872
Diminished intravascular volume from hemorrhage or fluid loss from vomiting or fever can similarly be replaced by gradual incremental boluses of crystalloid.

482
01:02:40,872 --> 01:02:48,647
For labor analgesia with neuraxial analgesia, crystalloid solutions are infused slowly in graded amounts .

483
01:02:48,647 --> 01:02:59,335
Pulmonary Edema Women with severe preeclampsia who develop pulmonary edema most often do so postpartum (Cunningham, 1986, 2012).

484
01:02:59,335 --> 01:03:10,122
With pulmonary edema in the eclamptic woman, aspiration of gastric contents, which may be the result of convulsions, anesthesia, or oversedation, should be excluded.

485
01:03:10,122 --> 01:03:22,060
Otherwise, in women with severe preeclampsia, three common causes of pulmonary edema are pulmonary capillary permeability edema, cardiogenic edema, or their combination.

486
01:03:22,060 --> 01:03:26,160
First, in women with preeclampsia, both increased

487
01:03:26,308 --> 01:03:35,883
capillary permeability and greater extravascular fluid oncotic pressure are found (Brown, 1989; Øian, 1986).

488
01:03:35,833 --> 01:03:41,583
If intravenous fluid replacement is vigorous, pulmonary congestion can follow.

489
01:03:41,583 --> 01:03:50,695
Second, in some women, pulmonary edema may be caused by ventricular failure from increased afterload that results from severe hypertension.

490
01:03:50,695 --> 01:03:58,520
Such pulmonary edema from ventricular failure is more common in morbidly obese women and in those with chronic hypertension.

491
01:03:58,520 --> 01:04:17,870
Invasive Hemodynamic Monitoring Knowledge concerning cardiovascular and hemodynamic pathophysiological alterations associated with severe preeclampsia–eclampsia has accrued from studies done using invasive monitoring and a pulmonary artery catheter (Fig. 40-5, p. 694).

492
01:04:17,870 --> 01:04:25,620
Two conditions frequently cited as indications are preeclampsia associated with oliguria or with pulmonary edema .

493
01:04:25,620 --> 01:04:32,283
Somewhat ironically, it is usually vigorous treatment of the former that results in most cases of the latter.

494
01:04:32,283 --> 01:04:38,308
The Task Force (2013) recommends against routine invasive monitoring.

495
01:04:38,308 --> 01:04:50,383
This is best reserved for women with severe preeclampsia and with accompanying cardiac disease, renal disease, or both or with refractory hypertension, oliguria, and pulmonary edema.

496
01:04:50,383 --> 01:04:59,083
Preliminary data from studies using a noninvasive cardiac monitoring system need to be verified before widespread clinical application .

497
01:04:59,083 --> 01:05:02,158
# Plasma Volume Expansion.

498
01:05:02,158 --> 01:05:14,695
Because the preeclampsia syndrome is associated with hemoconcentration, some have infused various fluids, starch polymers, albumin concentrates, or combinations to expand blood volume.

499
01:05:14,695 --> 01:05:25,945
Older studies describe serious complications—especially pulmonary edema—with volume expansion (Benedetti, 1985; Sibai, 1987b).

500
01:05:25,945 --> 01:05:36,808
The Amsterdam randomized study reported by Ganzevoort and coworkers (2005a,b) was a well-designed investigation done to evaluate volume expansion.

501
01:05:36,808 --> 01:05:44,633
A total of 216 women with severe preeclampsia were enrolled between 24 and 34 weeks’ gestation.

502
01:05:44,633 --> 01:05:54,508
The study included women whose preeclampsia was complicated by HELLP syndrome, eclampsia, pulmonary edema, or fetal-growth restriction.

503
01:05:54,508 --> 01:06:05,283
In the group randomly assigned to volume expansion, each woman was given 250 mL of 6-percent hydroxyethyl starch infused over 4 hours twice daily.

504
01:06:05,283 --> 01:06:11,345
Pregnancy outcomes were compared with a control group, and none of these were significantly different.

505
01:06:11,345 --> 01:06:19,220
Importantly, serious maternal morbidity and a substantive perinatal mortality rate accompanied their expectant management.

506
01:06:19,220 --> 01:06:22,458
# Analgesia and Anesthesia.

507
01:06:22,458 --> 01:06:30,595
During the past 25 years, the use of neuraxial analgesia for women with preeclampsia syndrome has proved ideal.

508
01:06:30,595 --> 01:06:38,970
Randomized studies attest to its safety, and these trials are fully described in Chapter 25 (p. 478).

509
01:06:38,970 --> 01:06:51,395
Initial problems with neuraxial analgesia included hypotension and diminished uterine perfusion caused by sympathetic blockade in preeclamptic women, who already have attenuated hypervolemia.

510
01:06:51,395 --> 01:07:08,608
However, slow induction of epidural analgesia with dilute solutions of anesthetic agents counter the need for rapid infusion of large volumes of crystalloid or colloid to prevent maternal hypotension (Hogg, 1999; Wallace, 1995).

511
01:07:08,608 --> 01:07:18,133
Importantly, epidural blockade avoids general anesthesia, in which the stimulation of tracheal intubation may cause sudden severe hypertension.

512
01:07:18,133 --> 01:07:25,108
Such blood pressure spikes can cause pulmonary edema, cerebral edema, or intracranial hemorrhage.

513
01:07:25,108 --> 01:07:33,333
Last, tracheal intubation may be particularly difficult and thus hazardous in women with airway edema due to preeclampsia .

514
01:07:33,333 --> 01:07:40,508
Judicious fluid administration is essential in women with severe preeclampsia who receive regional analgesia.

515
01:07:40,508 --> 01:07:49,183
Vigorous crystalloid infusion with epidural blockade in women with severe preeclampsia elevates pulmonary capillary wedge pressures .

516
01:07:49,183 --> 01:08:02,433
Aggressive volume replacement in preeclamptic women raises their risk for pulmonary edema, especially in the first 72 hours postpartum (Clark, 1985; Cotton, 1986a).

517
01:08:02,433 --> 01:08:08,745
Last, most cases of pharyngolaryngeal edema are related to aggressive volume therapy .

518
01:08:08,745 --> 01:08:18,708
In sum, general anesthesia, epidural analgesia, or combined spinal-epidural analgesia are acceptable for women severe preeclampsia

519
01:08:18,868 --> 01:08:23,493
if steps are taken to ensure a careful approach to the selected method.

520
01:08:23,443 --> 01:08:28,943
At Parkland Hospital, a gentle bolus is given accompanying epidural placement .

521
01:08:28,943 --> 01:08:31,743
# Blood Loss at Delivery.

522
01:08:31,743 --> 01:08:44,093
Hemoconcentration or lack of normal pregnancy-induced hypervolemia is an almost predictable feature of severe preeclampsia–eclampsia (Fig. 40-7, p. 696) .

523
01:08:44,093 --> 01:08:51,693
These women, who consequently lack normal pregnancy hypervolemia, may poorly tolerate even normal blood loss.

524
01:08:51,693 --> 01:09:02,105
Thus, an appreciable fall in blood pressure soon after delivery most often means excessive blood loss and not sudden resolution of vasospasm and endothelial damage.

525
01:09:02,105 --> 01:09:09,805
When oliguria follows delivery, the hematocrit should be evaluated frequently to help detect excessive blood loss.

526
01:09:09,805 --> 01:09:15,893
If identified, hemorrhage should be treated appropriately by crystalloid and blood transfusion.

527
01:09:15,893 --> 01:09:19,855
# Persistent Severe Postpartum Hypertension.

528
01:09:19,855 --> 01:09:24,455
Postpartum, 8 percent of women develop de novo hypertension .

529
01:09:24,455 --> 01:09:34,980
At times, controlling severe hypertension may be difficult or intravenous hydralazine or labetalol or oral immediate-release nifedipine are being used repeatedly.

530
01:09:34,980 --> 01:09:39,155
In these cases, oral maintenance regimens can be given.

531
01:09:39,155 --> 01:09:48,918
Examples include labetalol or another β-blocking agent; nifedipine extended release; amlodipine; or another calcium-channel blocking agent .

532
01:09:48,918 --> 01:09:56,968
Women so treated are less likely to require readmission (Stamilio, 2021; Wen, 2019).

533
01:09:56,968 --> 01:10:06,068
The Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2020a) has developed a check list for postpartum care of women with hypertensive disorders.

534
01:10:06,068 --> 01:10:21,130
Persistent hypertension is likely aggravated by pathological interstitial fluid that is now returning to the intravascular compartment, by underlying chronic hypertension, or by both (Sibai, 2012; Tan, 2002).

535
01:10:21,130 --> 01:10:31,330
Nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs do not aggravate postpartum hypertension (Anastasio, 2018; Penfield, 2019).

536
01:10:31,330 --> 01:10:46,980
In some women with chronic hypertension and leftventricular hypertrophy, severe postpartum hypertension can cause pulmonary edema from cardiac failure (Cunningham, 1986, 2019; Sibai, 1987a).

537
01:10:46,980 --> 01:10:55,405
Furosemide Severe hypertension persists depending on the onset and length of extracellular fluid mobilization and diuresis.

538
01:10:55,405 --> 01:11:02,355
Thus, it seems logical that furosemide-augmented diuresis might serve to hasten blood pressure control .

539
01:11:02,355 --> 01:11:19,068
In one randomized trial of 384 women with hypertensive disorders of pregnancy, a cohort that received furosemide postpartum had significantly lower blood pressures at 7 days—6 versus 14 percent— compared with women who received placebo .

540
01:11:19,068 --> 01:11:26,493
The need for antihypertensive treatment to be given at discharge in the furosemide group also was significantly reduced.

541
01:11:26,493 --> 01:11:35,293
In another randomized study, women with severe postpartum preeclampsia received nifedipine plus furosemide or nifedipine alone.

542
01:11:35,293 --> 01:11:44,368
The furosemide regimen significantly lowered the need for additional antihypertensive agents—8 versus 26 percent, respectively .

543
01:11:44,368 --> 01:11:49,593
Conversely, in one study, torsemide had no significant benefits .

544
01:11:49,593 --> 01:11:54,180
We use a simple method to estimate excessive extracellular fluid.

545
01:11:54,180 --> 01:12:02,018
The postpartum weight is compared with the most recent prenatal weight, either from the last clinic visit or from admission for delivery.

546
01:12:02,018 --> 01:12:12,993
Typically, soon after delivery, maternal weight should be reduced by at least 10 to 15 pounds depending on newborn and placental weight, amnionic fluid volume, and blood loss.

547
01:12:12,993 --> 01:12:30,193
Because of various interventions, especially intravenous crystalloid infusions given with labor epidural analgesia or during operative vaginal or cesarean delivery, women with severe preeclampsia often have an immediate postpartum weight in excess of their last prenatal weight.

548
01:12:30,193 --> 01:12:40,655
If this weight increase is associated with severe persistent postpartum hypertension, diuresis with intravenous furosemide can be helpful in controlling blood pressure.

549
01:12:40,655 --> 01:12:50,093
Thrombotic Microangiopathy Occasionally, women have an atypical syndrome in which severe preeclampsia–eclampsia persists despite delivery.

550
01:12:50,093 --> 01:12:57,718
Martin and associates (1995) described 18 such women whom they encountered during a 10-year period.

551
01:12:57,718 --> 01:13:02,205
They advocate single or multiple plasma exchange for these women.

552
01:13:02,205 --> 01:13:05,093
In some cases, 3 L of

553
01:13:05,260 --> 01:13:09,972
plasma was exchanged three times before a response was forthcoming.

554
01:13:09,922 --> 01:13:15,147
This is a 36- to 45-donor-unit exposure for each patient.

555
01:13:15,147 --> 01:13:25,197
Others have described plasma exchange performed in postpartum women with HELLP syndrome (Förster, 2002; Obeidat, 2002).

556
01:13:25,197 --> 01:13:35,847
In all of these cases, however, the distinction between HELLP syndrome and thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura or hemolytic uremic syndrome was not clear .

557
01:13:35,847 --> 01:13:57,147
In our experiences with more than 55,000 women with gestational hypertension among nearly 500,000 pregnancies cared for at Parkland Hospital through 2021, we have encountered very few women with persistent postpartum hypertension, thrombocytopenia, and renal dysfunction who were diagnosed as having a thrombotic microangiopathy .

558
01:13:57,147 --> 01:14:07,672
Reversible Cerebral Vasoconstriction Syndrome This is another cause of persistent hypertension, “thunderclap” headaches, seizures, and central nervous system findings.

559
01:14:07,672 --> 01:14:18,635
Reversible cerebral vasoconstriction syndrome is characterized by diffuse segmental constriction of cerebral arteries and may be associated with ischemic and hemorrhagic strokes.

560
01:14:18,635 --> 01:14:25,135
This syndrome has several inciting causes that include pregnancy and particularly preeclampsia .

561
01:14:25,135 --> 01:14:29,885
It is more frequent in women, and long-term sequelae are uncommon.

562
01:14:29,885 --> 01:14:37,460
However, in some cases, vasoconstriction may be so severe as to cause cerebral ischemia and infarction .

563
01:14:37,460 --> 01:14:42,572
We have encountered only a few women with a stroke cause by this arteriopathy .

564
01:14:42,572 --> 01:14:45,835
The most appropriate management is unclear .

565
01:14:45,835 --> 01:14:48,397
Long-Term Consequences.

566
01:14:48,397 --> 01:14:51,147
# Future Pregnancies.

567
01:14:51,147 --> 01:15:00,022
Women with preeclampsia during an index pregnancy are at risk for hypertensive and other disorders in subsequent pregnancies and later in life .

568
01:15:00,022 --> 01:15:10,485
In some, but not all women, the risk for hypertension is higher in the first 6 months postpartum (Giorgione, 2021; Mulder, 2021).

569
01:15:10,485 --> 01:15:24,647
Even in subsequent nonhypertensive pregnancies, women who had preterm preeclampsia have a higher risk for preterm birth and growth-restricted neonates (Connealy, 2014; Palatnik, 2016).

570
01:15:24,647 --> 01:15:32,285
Generally, the earlier preeclampsia is diagnosed during the index pregnancy, the greater the likelihood of recurrence.

571
01:15:32,285 --> 01:15:49,285
Women with preeclampsia near term had a recurrence risk of 23 percent, but nulliparas diagnosed with preeclampsia before 30 weeks had a recurrence risk as high as 40 percent (Bramham, 2011; Sibai 1986, 1991).

572
01:15:49,285 --> 01:15:57,335
As perhaps expected, women with HELLP syndrome have a substantive risk for recurrence in subsequent pregnancies.

573
01:15:57,335 --> 01:16:13,860
Even if HELLP syndrome does not recur with subsequent pregnancies, again incidences of preterm delivery, fetal-growth restriction, placental abruption, and cesarean delivery are increased (Habli, 2009; Malmström, 2020).

574
01:16:13,860 --> 01:16:16,997
# Cardiovascular Morbidity.

575
01:16:16,997 --> 01:16:28,685
The preeclampsia syndrome is also a marker for subsequent long-term cardiovascular morbidity (Hammad, 2020; Stuart, 2018; Wang, 2021).

576
01:16:28,685 --> 01:16:36,385
Thus, women with hypertension identified during pregnancy should be evaluated during the first several months postpartum.

577
01:16:36,385 --> 01:16:38,922
Table 41-10.

578
01:16:38,922 --> 01:16:55,160
Some Long-Term Consequences in Women with Preeclampsia Syndrome The Working Group of the NHBPEP (2000) concluded that hypertension attributable to pregnancy should resolve within 12 weeks of delivery, and hypertension persisting beyond this time is considered chronic .

579
01:16:55,160 --> 01:17:08,385
The Magpie Trial Follow-Up Collaborative Group (2007) reported that 20 percent of 3375 preeclamptic women seen at a median of 26 months postpartum had hypertension.

580
01:17:08,385 --> 01:17:17,085
From one metanaalysis, a 28-percent incidence of hypertension was found within 2 years of deliveries complicated by preeclampsia .

581
01:17:17,085 --> 01:17:36,010
From a Danish registry and after a mean of almost 15 years, the incidence of chronic hypertension was fivefold higher in those who had gestational hypertension, fourfold greater after mild preeclampsia, and sixfold higher after severe preeclampsia compared with women without hypertension in pregnancy .

582
01:17:36,010 --> 01:17:42,385
Any hypertension during pregnancy is a risk marker for cardiovascular morbidity in later life .

583
01:17:42,385 --> 01:17:50,210
As shown in data from Brouwers and coworkers (2018), the risk is greater in women with recurrent preeclampsia .

584
01:17:50,210 --> 01:17:55,422
In a study from Iceland, the prevalence of ischemic heart disease—24

585
01:17:55,588 --> 01:18:06,988
versus 15 percent, and of stroke—9.5 versus 6.5 percent, were increased in women who had gestational hypertension compared with normotensive controls .

586
01:18:06,938 --> 01:18:17,750
In a Swedish population study of more than 400,000 women, those with recurrent preeclampsia had systolic dysfunction and a greater incidence of ischemic heart disease .

587
01:18:17,750 --> 01:18:21,213
Diastolic dysfunction also is more common .

588
01:18:21,213 --> 01:18:32,025
Preeclampsia is also a risk for coronary artery calcification and idiopathic cardiomyopathy (Gammill, 2018; White, 2016).

589
01:18:32,025 --> 01:18:34,625
Table 41-11.

590
01:18:34,625 --> 01:18:52,563
Long-Term Cardiovascular Morbidities After Recurrent Preeclampsia Compared with a Single Pregnancy with Preeclampsia and a Subsequent Normal Pregnancy Women who have preeclampsia and who develop chronic hypertension later in life have an increased ventricular mass index before they become hypertensive .

591
01:18:52,563 --> 01:19:02,100
Last, in at least some women with prior preeclampsia, hypertensive cardiovascular pathologies appear to have begun near the time of their own births.

592
01:19:02,100 --> 01:19:07,825
A similar phenomenon is associated with preterm birth and with fetal-growth disorders.

593
01:19:07,825 --> 01:19:21,788
Other cofactors or comorbidities are related to acquisition of these longterm adverse outcomes (Gastrich, 2012; Harskamp, 2007; Hermes, 2012; Spaan, 2012).

594
01:19:21,788 --> 01:19:35,188
These include the metabolic syndrome, diabetes, obesity, dyslipidemia, and atherosclerosis (Catzov, 2021; Cho, 2019; Kajantie, 2017).

595
01:19:35,188 --> 01:19:41,375
Women with pregnancy-associated hypertension are at increased risk for type 2 diabetes .

596
01:19:41,375 --> 01:19:51,225
Preeclampsia predisposes for later diabetic retinopathy and retinal detachment (Auger, 2017; Beharier, 2016).

597
01:19:51,225 --> 01:19:53,700
# Renal Sequelae.

598
01:19:53,700 --> 01:19:57,788
Preeclampsia is a marker for subsequent renal disease.

599
01:19:57,788 --> 01:20:03,038
Almost 15 percent of women with prior preeclampsia have renal dysfunction .

600
01:20:03,038 --> 01:20:11,375
This may be related to AKI associated with preeclampsia (Novotny, 2020; Rodriquez, 2021).

601
01:20:11,375 --> 01:20:19,225
In a Danish study with more than a million women, the chronic renal disease rate was sixfold greater in those who had preeclampsia .

602
01:20:19,225 --> 01:20:29,538
These data need to be considered in light of the findings that 15 to 20 percent of women with preeclampsia who undergo renal biopsy have evidence of chronic renal disease .

603
01:20:29,538 --> 01:20:40,888
In another long-term study, women with prior preeclampsia were significantly more likely to be chronically hypertensive—55 versus 7 percent —compared with control women.

604
01:20:40,888 --> 01:20:47,500
They also had higher peripheral vascular and renovascular resistance and decreased renal blood flow.

605
01:20:47,500 --> 01:20:52,088
These data do not permit conclusions as to cause versus effect .

606
01:20:52,088 --> 01:20:55,313
# Central Nervous System Sequelae.

607
01:20:55,313 --> 01:21:00,988
Eclamptic seizures were once believed to have no significant long-term CNS sequelae.

608
01:21:00,988 --> 01:21:08,250
However, this likely is not the case (Bergman, 2021c; Theilen, 2016).

609
01:21:08,250 --> 01:21:18,275
Recall that almost all eclamptic women have multifocal areas of perivascular edema, and approximately a fourth also have areas of cerebral infarction .

610
01:21:18,275 --> 01:21:30,513
In several long-term studies in women with severe preeclampsia and eclampsia, white-matter lesions in the brain that followed eclamptic convulsions persist (Aukes, 2009, 2012).

611
01:21:30,513 --> 01:21:43,963
Specifically, with magnetic resonance (MR) imaging, 40 percent of women with prior eclampsia had more numerous and larger aggregate white-matter lesions compared with 17 percent of normotensive control women.

612
01:21:43,963 --> 01:21:49,663
These white-matter lesions were also found in women with preeclampsia without convulsions .

613
01:21:49,663 --> 01:21:56,638
Others found temporal lobe white-matter changes and reduced cortical volume in women with prior preeclampsia .

614
01:21:56,638 --> 01:22:09,388
In some, but not all studies, women with prior eclampsia had subjectively impaired cognitive functioning and increased risk for dementia (Basit, 2018; Elharram, 2018).

615
01:22:09,388 --> 01:22:13,988
They also had a modest increase in the frequency of seizure disorders .

616
01:22:13,988 --> 01:22:21,238
In another study, women with prior eclampsia had lower vision-related quality of life compared with control women .

